2 CONT Karl-Henrik Karlsson: Duke Bengt Algotsson's ancestral lineage Table of contents Table of contents ... 2 Duke Bengt Algotsson's ancestral lineage ... 3 Appendix A Supplements and amendments ... 5 A.1 Supplements ... 5 A.2 Source l
2 CONC ist. .. 5 A.3 Genealogy ... 6 A.4 Person and place register ... 7 Appendix B Original edition in scanned form ... 8 SGS1B2-A Swedish Karl-Henrik Karlsson: Duke Bengt Algotsson's ancestral family.  Duke Bengt Algotsson's ancestral 
2 CONC family Karl-Henrik Karlsson The darkness that has long rested over King Magnus Eriksson's favorite, the well-known Duke Bengt Algotsson's family, has begun to dissipate in recent decades. It has been shown that he was far from bei
2 CONC ng of low birth, as previously claimed, belonged to one of Sweden's most high-born families, and that through his grandmother Mrs. Ingegärd Svantepolksdotter he could lead his family up to both the Sverker royal family and the Dan
2 CONC ish king Valdemar Victory.  On the father's, however, his family could not be traced further back than to his grandfather Brynjulf ​​Bengtsson, who is mentioned in the Nordic Family Book, it is admittedly highly probable that this
2 CONC  Brynjulf ​​Bengtsson was a younger son of Östgötalagmannen Bengt Magnusson. However, since nothing other than the common coat of arms, the lion, speaks in favor of this assumption and also Bengt Magnusson's family was attached to
2 CONC  Östergötland, but Duke Bengts again at Vestergötland, one should be fully entitled to throw this whole derivation over the table as less likely, if namely at the same time a more plausible genealogy can be demonstrated. Duke Beng
2 CONC t, his father Algot Brynjulfsson, uncle Knut Brynjulfsson and aunts Birgitta Brynjulfsdotter, gm the knight Greger Magnusson (2 slanted beams), and Katarina Brynjulfsdotter gm the knight Ulf Håkansson (Läma), carried a lion in the
2 CONC  shield, and then the lion image Bf Hafridsson, 2 who is named between the years 1286 and 1307, should be reason to investigate, if no relationship between them can be demonstrated. As children of Bengt Hafridsson or his widow Mar
2 CONC gareta are mentioned in separate letters Folke, Ramfrid, g. roses. Of these, Ramfrid Bengtsdotter lived the longest and in her last will of the year she mentions as a cousin Mr. Folke Knutsson, domprost in Skara, who is also menti
2 CONC oned among her heirs, 1 Illustrated Swedish history 2, page Sv. Sig. fr. Middle Ages 3: 103, 307, 308, 714; Sv. RP n Sv. Hist. Journal 1885, p Sv. RP n Sv. D. 3, n Swedish Genealogical Society, Vadstena SGS1B2-A 3 Of these, Ramfri
2 CONC d Bengtsdotter lived the longest and in her last will of the year she mentions as a cousin Mr. Folke Knutsson, domprost in Skara, who is also mentioned among her heirs, 1 Illustrated Swedish history 2, page Sv. Sig. fr. Middle Age
2 CONC s 3: 103, 307, 308, 714; Sv. RP n Sv. Hist. Journal 1885, p Sv. RP n Sv. D. 3, n Swedish Genealogical Society, Vadstena SGS1B2-A 3 Of these, Ramfrid Bengtsdotter lived the longest and in her last will of the year she mentions as a
2 CONC  cousin Mr. Folke Knutsson, domprost in Skara, who is also mentioned among her heirs, 1 Illustrated Swedish history 2, page Sv. Sig. fr. Middle Ages 3: 103, 307, 308, 714; Sv. RP n Sv. Hist. Journal 1885, p Sv. RP n Sv. D. 3, n Sw
2 CONC edish Genealogical Society, Vadstena SGS1B2-A
2 CONT
2 CONT Duke Bengt Algotsson's ancestral family. The darkness that has long rested over King Magnus Eriksson's favorite, the family of the well-known Duke Bengt Algotsson, has in recent decades begun to dissipate. It has been shown that h
2 CONC e was far from being of low birth, as previously claimed, belonged to one of Sweden's most high-born families, and that through his grandmother Mrs. Ingegärd Svantepolksdotter he could lead his family up to both the Sverker royal 
2 CONC family and the Danish King Valdemar Sejer.1 On the other hand, the father has not been able to trace his lineage further back than to his grandfather Brynjulf ​​Bengtsson, who is mentioned in the Nordic Family Book. . Then, howeve
2 CONC r, nothing but the common weapon image, the lion, speaks for this assumption and also Bengt Magnusson's family was attached to Östergötland, but Duke Bengts again at Vestergötland, one should be fully entitled to throw this whole 
2 CONC derivation over the table as less likely, if at the same time a more acceptable family management can be demonstrated. Duke Bengt, his father Algot Brynjulfsson, uncle Knut Brynjulfsson and aunts Birgitta Brynjulfsdotter, gm the k
2 CONC night Greger Magnusson (2 slanted beams), and Katarina Brynjulfsdotter gm the knight Ulf Håkansson (Läma), carried a lion in the shield, and then the lion image of Vest lagmannen Bengt Haf- 1 Illustrated Swedish history 2, p. 24. 
2 CONC one should be fully entitled to throw this whole derivation over the table as less likely, if at the same time a more plausible lineage can be demonstrated. Duke Bengt, his father Algot Brynjulfsson, uncle Knut Brynjulfsson and au
2 CONC nts Birgitta Brynjulfsdotter, gm the knight Greger Magnusson (2 slanted beams), and Katarina Brynjulfsdotter gm the knight Ulf Håkansson (Läma), carried a lion in the shield, and then the lion image of Vest lagmannen Bengt Haf- 1 
2 CONC Illustrated Swedish history 2, p. 24. one should be fully entitled to throw this whole derivation over the table as less likely, if at the same time a more plausible lineage can be demonstrated. Duke Bengt, his father Algot Brynju
2 CONC lfsson, uncle Knut Brynjulfsson and aunts Birgitta Brynjulfsdotter, gm the knight Greger Magnusson (2 slanted beams), and Katarina Brynjulfsdotter gm the knight Ulf Håkansson (Läma), carried a lion in the shield, and then the lion
2 CONC  image of Vest lagmannen Bengt Haf- 1 Illustrated Swedish history 2, p. 24. och då lejonbilden äfven föres af Vestgöta-lagmannen Bengt Haf- 1 Illustrerad Svensk historia 2, sid. 24. och då lejonbilden äfven föres af Vestgöta-lagma
2 CONC nnen Bengt Haf- 1 Illustrerad Svensk historia 2, sid. 24.
2 CONT
2 CONT I6I ridsson, 1 who is named between the years 1286 and 1307, should be reason to investigate, if no kinship between them can be demonstrated. As children of Bengt Hafridsson or his widow Margareta are mentioned in separate letters
2 CONC  Folke, Ramfrid, g. roses. 2 Of these, Ramfrid Bengtsdotter lived the longest and in her last will of the year she mentions as a cousin Mr. Folke Knutsson, domprost in Skara, who is also mentioned among her heirs, Magnus Gregersso
2 CONC n and Algot Bengtsson. Folke Knutsson was the son of Mrs. Ramfrid's sister Kristina. Magnus Gregersson was the son of the knight Greger Magnusson (2 slanted beams) and Birgitta Brynjulfsdotter (lion) a daughter of Brynjulf ​​Bengt
2 CONC sson and thus aunt of Duke Bengt Algotsson (lion). Algot Bengtsson was a lawyer in Vester-, götland, carried a grip head in the weapon and was the grandson of the well-known lawyer Algot Brynjulfsson. That he too was related to Du
2 CONC ke Bengt's ancestors appears from a letter issued in 1322 by Mrs. Ingegärd Svantepolksdotter on Händelö, 4 in which he and Greger Magnusson (2 slanted beams) and Mrs. Ingegärd's sons Knut and Algot Brynjulfsson (lions) are mention
2 CONC ed as seals. For the relationship between Mrs. Ramfrid Bengtsdotter herself and Brynjulf ​​Bengtsson's children further speaks the fact that Mr. Gregers Magnusson (2 slanted beams) and Knut and Algot Brynjulfssöner sealed (1332) a
2 CONC  letter issued by her.5 Then thus both Bengt Hafridsson and Duke Bengt Algotsson and his ancestral lineage in the arms carry a lion, as they are both most closely tied to Vestergötland, furthermore Bengt Hafridsson's daughter Mrs.
2 CONC  Ramfrid Bengtsdotter and Duke Bengts father have common relatives and then finally Mrs. Ramfrid herself seems to be related to Duke Bengts father, it seems to me most likely that Duke Bengts grandfather Brynjulf ​​Bengtsson was a
2 CONC  son of Vestgöta-lagmannen Bengt Hafridsson and older brother of Mrs. Ramfrid Bengtsdotter. Concerning Bengt Hafridsson's and Bengt Algotsson's family connections in general, I refer to an essay by me in Sv. Historisk tidskrift p.
2 CONC  172 and to H. Hildebrand's genealogy in Illustrated Swedish History, 2, p. 24. KHK 1 Sv. Sig. fr. Middle Ages 3: 103, 307, 308, 714; Sv. RP n Sv. Hist. Journal 1885, p Sv. RP n Sv. D. 3, n Sv. D. 4. n it seems to me most probable
2 CONC  that Duke Bengt's grandfather Brynjulf ​​Bengtsson was a son of the Vestgöta lawyer Bengt Hafridsson and an older brother of Mrs. Ramfrid Bengtsdotter. Concerning Bengt Hafridsson's and Bengt Algotsson's family connections in gen
2 CONC eral, I refer to an essay by me in Sv. Historisk tidskrift p. 172 and to H. Hildebrand's genealogy in Illustrated Swedish History, 2, p. 24. KHK 1 Sv. Sig. fr. Middle Ages 3: 103, 307, 308, 714; Sv. RP n Sv. Hist. Journal 1885, p 
